istic Spilling
What happens if we remove nodes of degree $< K$ until every remaining node in $G$ has $\deg(v) \ge K$?
* **Chaitin (1981):** Immediately marks a node $v$ with lowest spill-cost-to-degree ratio $\frac{\text{SpillCost}(v)}{\deg(v)}$ as **spilled**, removes it from $G$, and rewrites the program to spill $v$ to the stack.
* **Briggs et al. (1989) Optimistic Coloring:** Notices that even if $\deg(v) \ge K$ (say, $K = 3$ and $v$ has $3$ neighbors in a 4-cycle), two or more of $v$'s neighbors might receive the **same color** during the Select phase, leaving a free color for $v$!
  Instead of immediately spilling $v$, Briggs pushes $v$ onto the coloring stack anyway (marking it as a *potential spill*) and continues simplifying. Only during the **Select** phase (when popping $v$ off the stack), if $v$'s neighbors actually occupy all $K$ distinct colors, does Briggs mark $v$ as an **actual spill**!

#### Iterated Register Coalescing (George and Appel, 1996)
Copy instructions (`v = u`) arise from SSA destruction, calling conventions, and user assignments. If `u` and `v` do not interfere ($(u, v) \notin E$), we would like to **coalesce** them into a single node `uv` in $G$ so they receive the same physical register and the move instruction `v = u` can be deleted!

However, reckless coalescing can make a $K$-colorable graph **uncolorable** because the merged node `uv` has the union of `u`'s and `v`'s neighbors, potentially raising its degree to $\ge K$. George and Appel solved this by interleaving Simplify and Safe Coalescing using two provably safe coalescing criteria (neither of which can ever turn a $K$-colorable graph into a non-simplifiable graph):
1. **Briggs Conservative Coalescing Criterion:** Nodes $u$ and $v$ can be safely coalesced if the merged node $uv$ will have **fewer than $K$ neighbors of significant degree** (where a neighbor $w$ has significant degree if $\deg(w) \ge K$).
2. **George Conservative Coalescing Criterion:** (Used when coalescing a virtual register $u$ with a pre-colored physical register $v$): $u$ and $v$ can be safely coalesced if for every neighbor $t$ of $u$, either $t$ already interferes with $v$, OR $\deg(t) < K$.

```
               +---> [ Build Interference Graph ] <---+
               |                  |                   |
               |                  v                   |
               |         [ Simplify (< K) ]           |
               |           |            ^             |
               |  No < K   |            | Coalesced   |
               |           v            |             |
               |     [ Coalesce (Briggs/George) ]     |
               |           |                          |
               |  Stuck    v                          |
               |     [ Freeze MoveEdge ]              |
               |           |                          |
               |  Stuck    v                          |
               |   [ Potential Spill (>= K) ]         |
               |           |                          |
               |  Empty G  v                          |
               |     [ Select Colors ]                |
               |       /           \                  |
               |  Actual Spills     All Colored!      |
               +--- [ Rewrite IR ]      v             |
                                     ( DONE )
```

*(Note on SSA Register Allocation: **Hack, Grund, and Goos (2006)** proved that the interference graph of any program in strict SSA form is a **Chordal Graph**! For chordal graphs, the chromatic number equals the size of the largest clique—i.e., the maximum number of variables simultaneously live at any single program point—and optimal $K$-coloring without spilling can be found in $\mathcal{O}(|V| + |E|)$ time via **Lexicographic Breadth-First Search** without ever needing an explicit $V \times V$ interference matrix!)*

### 9.2 Linear Scan Register Allocation (Poletto & Sarkar)

In Just-In-Time (JIT) compilers and fast baseline compile modes (`-O0`), constructing an $\mathcal{O}(|V|^2)$ interference graph and iterating liveness/rewriting passes is too slow. **Poletto and Sarkar (1999)** introduced **Linear Scan Register Allocation**, which allocates registers in a single greedy pass over sorted **Live Intervals** in $\mathcal{O}(V \log V)$ time, achieving within $5\text{–}10\%$ of Graph Coloring's runtime code quality while running $5\times\text{–}10\times$ faster.

#### Definition of Live Intervals
Number all instructions in the linearized CFG from $1$ to $N$. The **Live Interval** of virtual register $v$ is the contiguous range $[s_v, e_v]$ where $s_v$ is the first instruction index where $v$ becomes live and $e_v$ is the last instruction index where $v$ is live (including any loop back-edge extensions if $v$ is live across a loop header).

### 9.3 Concrete Implementation: Both Chaitin-Briggs and Linear Scan in C

Below are complete, self-contained C implementations of **both** the Chaitin-Briggs Graph Coloring Allocator and Poletto-Sarkar's Linear Scan Allocator.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

// ============================================================================
// PART A: Chaitin-Briggs Optimistic Graph Coloring Register Allocator (K <= 64)
// ============================================================================

#define MAX_VREGS 64

typedef struct {
    int num_nodes;
    int K; // Number of physical registers
    uint64_t adj[MAX_VREGS]; // Bitmatrix adjacency list for interference graph
    double spill_cost[MAX_VREGS];
    int color[MAX_VREGS];    // Assigned register 0..K-1, or -1 if spilled
} InterferenceGraph;

static inline int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    int c = 0;
    while (x) { x &= x - 1; c++; }
    return c;
}

bool chaitin_briggs_color(InterferenceGraph *g) {
    uint64_t active_mask = (g->num_nodes == 64) ? ~0ULL : ((1ULL << g->num_nodes) - 1ULL);
    int stack[MAX_VREGS];
    int stack_top = 0;

    // Phase 1: Simplify & Optimistic Potential Spill Selection
    while (active_mask != 0ULL) {
        int chosen = -1;

        // 1a. Look for any active node with current degree < K (Kempe Simplify)
        for (int v = 0; v < g->num_nodes; v++) {
            if (!(active_mask & (1ULL << v))) continue;
            int deg = popcount64(g->adj[v] & active_mask);
            if (deg < g->K) {
                chosen = v;
                break;
            }
        }

        // 1b. If all remaining nodes have degree >= K, pick lowest spill_cost / degree
        if (chosen == -1) {
            double best_metric = 1e30;
            for (int v = 0; v < g->num_nodes; v++) {
                if (!(active_mask & (1ULL << v))) continue;
                int deg = popcount64(g->adj[v] & active_mask);
                double metric = g->spill_cost[v] / (double)deg;
                if (metric < best_metric) {
                    best_metric = metric;
                    chosen = v;
                }
            }
        }

        // Push chosen node onto stack and remove from active subgraph
        stack[stack_top++] = chosen;
        active_mask &= ~(1ULL << chosen);
    }

    // Phase 2: Select Colors (Pop from stack and assign lowest available color)
    for (int i = 0; i < g->num_nodes; i++) g->color[i] = -1;
    bool all_colored = true;

    while (stack_top > 0) {
        int v = stack[--stack_top];
        uint64_t used_colors = 0ULL;

        uint64_t neighbors = g->adj[v];
        for (int u = 0; u < g->num_nodes; u++) {
            if ((neighbors & (1ULL << u)) && g->color[u] != -1) {
                used_colors |= (1ULL << g->color[u]);
            }
        }

        // Find lowest color c in 0..K-1 not in used_colors
        int assigned = -1;
        for (int c = 0; c < g->K; c++) {
            if (!(used_colors & (1ULL << c))) {
                assigned = c;
                break;
            }
        }

        g->color[v] = assigned;
        if (assigned == -1) {
            all_colored = false; // Actual spill required for virtual register v!
        }
    }
    return all_colored;
}

// ============================================================================
// PART B: Poletto & Sarkar Linear Scan Register Allocator
// ============================================================================

typedef struct {
    int vreg_id;
    int start;     // First instruction index where vreg is live
    int end;       // Last instruction index where vreg is live
    int phys_reg;  // Assigned physical register (0..R-1) or -1 if spilled
    int spill_slot;// Assigned stack frame spill offset if spilled
} LiveInterval;

static int cmp_by_start(const void *a, const void *b) {
    const LiveInterval *ia = (const LiveInterval *)a;
    const LiveInterval *ib = (const LiveInterval *)b;
    return (ia->start != ib->start) ? (ia->start - ib->start) : (ia->end - ib->end);
}

void linear_scan_allocate(LiveInterval *intervals, int n, int num_phys_regs) {
    // 1. Sort intervals in order of increasing start point
    qsort(intervals, n, sizeof(LiveInterval), cmp_by_start);

    // Active list holds pointers to intervals currently in registers, sorted by increasing 'end'
    LiveInterval **active = malloc(num_phys_regs * sizeof(LiveInterval *));
    int active_count = 0;

    // Free physical register pool
    bool *reg_free = malloc(num_phys_regs * sizeof(bool));
    for (int r = 0; r < num_phys_regs; r++) reg_free[r] = true;

    int next_spill_slot = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        LiveInterval *cur = &intervals[i];
        cur->phys_reg = -1;
        cur->spill_slot = -1;

        // 2. ExpireOldIntervals(cur): Free registers of active intervals that ended before cur->start
        int keep_idx = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < active_count; j++) {
            if (active[j]->end < cur->start) {
                reg_free[active[j]->phys_reg] = true; // Return register to free pool
            } else {
                active[keep_idx++] = active[j];
            }
        }
        active_count = keep_idx;

        // 3. Allocate or SpillAtInterval(cur)
        if (active_count == num_phys_regs) {
            // All registers are busy! Compare cur->end with the active interval ending furthest in the future
            LiveInterval *spill_candidate = active[active_count - 1];
            if (spill_candidate->end > cur->end) {
                // Steal physical register from spill_candidate and spill spill_candidate
                cur->phys_reg = spill_candidate->phys_reg;
                spill_candidate->phys_reg = -1;
                spill_candidate->spill_slot = next_spill_slot++;
                active_count--; // Remove spill_candidate from active
            } else {
                // Spill cur itself
                cur->spill_slot = next_spill_slot++;
                continue;
            }
        } else {
            // Grab a free physical register
            for (int r = 0; r < num_phys_regs; r++) {
                if (reg_free[r]) {
                    reg_free[r] = false;
                    cur->phys_reg = r;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Insert cur into active list keeping active sorted by increasing 'end'
        int pos = active_count;
        while (pos > 0 && active[pos - 1]->end > cur->end) {
            active[pos] = active[pos - 1];
            pos--;
        }
        active[pos] = cur;
        active_count++;
    }

    free(active);
    free(reg_free);
}
```

